-- Q880: SOC Engagements [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Management''s written assertion about system description and controls","The service auditor''s independent opinion on the controls","The user entity''s formal representation to regulators","The regulatory compliance certificate issued by the board"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320), management of the service organization provides a written assertion that its system description is fairly presented and that controls were suitably designed and operated effectively. The service auditor examines this assertion and issues an independent opinion.
Wrong (B): The auditor''s opinion is the independent evaluation issued after examining management''s assertion, not the assertion itself.
Wrong (C): User entities may provide representations, but the SOC 1 assertion is made by the service organization''s management.
Wrong (D): No regulatory compliance certificate is part of the SOC 1 reporting framework.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 880;

-- Q881: SOC Engagements [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The subservice organization must always be excluded from the engagement scope","Either the inclusive method or the carve-out method may be used to address it","Subservice organizations are only relevant in SOC 1 engagements, not SOC 2","The user entity bears sole responsibility for auditing the subservice organization"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AT-C Section 320 and the AICPA Guide: SOC 2 Reporting, subservice organizations are addressed through either the inclusive method (included in scope and tested) or the carve-out method (excluded with a description of expected controls). Both approaches are valid under SSAE 18.
Wrong (A): Exclusion is not mandatory; the inclusive method allows full inclusion of subservice organization controls in the engagement scope.
Wrong (C): Subservice organizations are relevant in both SOC 1 and SOC 2 engagements under AT-C Section 320.
Wrong (D): The service auditor, not the user entity, determines how to address subservice organizations within the SOC engagement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 881;

-- Q884: SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Availability (system uptime commitments)","Confidentiality (restricted information protection)","Security (Common Criteria for system protection)","Privacy (personal information governance)"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Security, also known as the Common Criteria, is the foundational category required in every SOC 2 engagement per the AICPA Trust Services Criteria (TSP Section 100). It addresses protection of information and systems against unauthorized access and damage.
Wrong (A): Availability is an optional criterion selected based on the service organization''s commitments regarding system uptime.
Wrong (B): Confidentiality is an optional criterion addressing protection of information designated as confidential.
Wrong (D): Privacy is an optional criterion covering personal information handling and is not required in every engagement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 884;

-- Q885: SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Security (protection against unauthorized access)","Availability (system uptime and usability)","Confidentiality (restricted information handling)","Processing Integrity (complete, accurate, timely processing)"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Processing Integrity addresses whether system processing is complete, valid, accurate, timely, and authorized per the AICPA Trust Services Criteria. It ensures that transactions such as automated clearing calculations produce reliable outputs.
Wrong (A): Security addresses protection against unauthorized access, disclosure, and damage, not processing accuracy.
Wrong (B): Availability addresses whether the system is operational and usable as committed, not whether outputs are correct.
Wrong (C): Confidentiality addresses protection of information designated as confidential, not processing completeness or accuracy.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 885;

-- Q886: SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Background checks and screening for new employees","Redundant infrastructure with automated failover and uptime SLAs","Encryption of sensitive data stored at rest","Physical access badge controls to the server room"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Redundant infrastructure with automated failover and defined SLAs is most relevant to the Availability criteria under the AICPA Trust Services Criteria (TSP Section 100, criterion A1.2). These controls ensure the system can withstand component failures and meet uptime commitments.
Wrong (A): Background checks relate to the Security criteria because they address personnel trustworthiness, not system uptime.
Wrong (C): Encryption of data at rest relates to the Confidentiality or Security criteria, not Availability.
