-- Q890: SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept the existing report since Security and Availability imply Processing Integrity coverage","Request expanded SOC 2 scope or perform independent audit procedures for that criterion","Issue a qualified opinion on the user entity''s financial statements immediately","Ignore the gap because SOC 2 reports are voluntary and not legally required"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the AICPA Trust Services Criteria (TSP Section 100), each criterion addresses distinct control objectives. Coverage of Security and Availability does not extend to Processing Integrity. The user auditor must obtain assurance through requesting expanded scope or performing independent testing.
Wrong (A): Each Trust Services criterion is independent; Security and Availability do not imply Processing Integrity coverage.
Wrong (C): A gap in SOC 2 scope does not automatically require a qualified opinion; the auditor must first assess the impact and perform alternative procedures.
Wrong (D): The voluntary nature of SOC 2 does not eliminate the user auditor''s responsibility to obtain sufficient assurance over relevant controls.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 890;

-- Q894: SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Evaluate the qualification''s impact, test compensating controls, and expand substantive procedures","Disregard the qualification because it covers only one-third of the reporting period","Automatically issue a qualified opinion on the user entity''s financial statements","Request the service organization to reissue the report without the qualification"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AU-C Section 402 (Audit Considerations Relating to Service Organizations), the user auditor must assess the nature and duration of the control failure, test compensating controls at the user entity, and determine whether expanded substantive procedures provide sufficient evidence.
Wrong (B): A four-month control failure covering one-third of the period is significant and cannot be disregarded based on duration alone.
Wrong (C): A qualification in the SOC report does not automatically result in a qualified opinion on the user entity''s financial statements.
Wrong (D): The user auditor cannot request reissuance; the qualification reflects the service auditor''s findings about actual control deficiencies.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 894;

-- Q895: SOC Reporting and Trust Services Criteria [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Uses fundamentally different attestation standards than SOC 2 engagements","Provides an entity-wide cybersecurity risk management examination, not a service-specific review","Is available exclusively to government agencies and public sector entities","Does not require a CPA firm to perform the examination engagement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AICPA''s SOC for Cybersecurity guidance (AT-C Section 205), this framework provides an entity-wide examination of the organization''s cybersecurity risk management program, whereas SOC 2 focuses on controls for a specific system or service. It uses AICPA description criteria rather than Trust Services Criteria.
Wrong (A): Both SOC 2 and SOC for Cybersecurity use AICPA attestation standards; the difference is scope and criteria, not the standards themselves.
Wrong (C): SOC for Cybersecurity is available to any organization, not restricted to government agencies.
Wrong (D): SOC for Cybersecurity requires a CPA to perform the examination, consistent with all AICPA attestation engagements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 895;

-- Q1050: IT General Controls [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Approve both changes for simultaneous deployment to minimize window duration","Evaluate combined risk and interaction, then sequence or defer one change","Reject both changes since two changes in one window always violate policy","Delegate the decision to the database administrator with the most expertise"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ITIL 4 Section 5.2 (Change Enablement), the CAB evaluates proposed changes for risk, impact, and resource requirements. When two changes affect the same environment, the CAB should assess their combined risk and either sequence them with rollback checkpoints or defer one to a separate window.
Wrong (A): Simultaneous deployment of interacting changes increases risk by making it difficult to isolate the cause of any failure.
Wrong (C): ITIL does not prohibit multiple changes in one window; it requires risk-based evaluation of each change and their interactions.
Wrong (D): The CAB is the authorization body for change decisions; delegating to a single technical resource bypasses the advisory process.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1050;

-- Q1213: SOC Testing Controls [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A deviation is any control departure; an exception is a deviation deemed a control objective failure","A deviation is a minor error while an exception is a major error in control execution","There is no meaningful distinction because the terms are interchangeable in SOC reporting","A deviation occurs only in Type I reports while an exception occurs only in Type II reports"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the AICPA Guide: SOC 2 Reporting Section 4 (Evaluating Deviations), a deviation is any instance where a control did not operate as described for a specific test item. An exception is a deviation the practitioner has evaluated and concluded represents a failure to meet the control objective. Not every deviation rises to exception status.
Wrong (B): The distinction is not based on severity (minor vs. major) but on whether the deviation constitutes a control objective failure.
Wrong (C): The terms have distinct meanings in SOC reporting; using them interchangeably is incorrect.
Wrong (D): Both deviations and exceptions can occur in Type II reports; the distinction is not report-type dependent.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1213;

-- Q1214: SOC Testing Controls [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Whether the numerical pass rate alone determines control effectiveness","Whether the auditor''s opinion must automatically be qualified for deviations","Whether the sample should always be expanded to exactly double its size","Whether it reflects a design or operating issue and whether compensating controls exist"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): When deviations are found, the auditor must first evaluate whether the deviation reflects a control design problem or an operating effectiveness failure, and whether compensating controls mitigate the risk. This assessment drives subsequent audit decisions per the AICPA Guide: SOC 2 Reporting.
Wrong (A): Pass rates must be considered alongside the nature and cause of deviations, not in isolation.
Wrong (B): Not all deviations automatically require opinion qualification; the auditor must assess significance first.
Wrong (C): Sample expansion is one possible response but is not automatic or always doubled; it depends on the nature of findings.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1214;

