-- Q3457: IT Infrastructure and Architecture [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["56-bit DES keys can be brute-forced, exposing financial data to interception","DES encryption operates too quickly to provide meaningful security protection","VPN concentrators should never use any form of encryption for connections","DES is deprecated only for government use and remains acceptable commercially"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per NIST SP 800-175B (Guideline for Using Cryptographic Standards), DES with 56-bit keys is deprecated because modern computing power makes brute-force attacks feasible. NIST requires minimum AES-128 or AES-256 for protecting sensitive data in transit, including VPN connections.
Wrong (B): DES''s speed is not the security concern; the vulnerability is that its 56-bit key space is computationally trivial to exhaust.
Wrong (C): VPN concentrators must use encryption; the issue is using a deprecated algorithm, not encryption itself.
Wrong (D): NIST deprecation of DES applies to all uses, both government and commercial, without exception.$EXPL$
